{
    "title": "Takes of Marine Mammals Incidental to Specified Activities; Taking Marine Mammals Incidental to Military Readiness Activities in the Hawaii-California Training and Testing Study Area",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 17, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-17/pdf/2025-23088.pdf",
    "summary": "NOAA has finalized a seven\u2011year rule that lets the Navy, Coast Guard, and Army continue training and testing between Hawaii and California, while setting new safeguards to reduce harm to whales, dolphins, and seals. The decision covers sonar use, in\u2011water explosions, pile driving, missile and target launches, and vessel movements from December 2025 through December 2032.\n\nWhat\u2019s in the rule: It allows limited, incidental harm to marine mammals during these activities, with strict guardrails. Crews must follow clear slow\u2011down and shut\u2011down zones around animals; avoid explosives in sensitive places and seasons; and use \u201csoft starts\u201d for impact pile driving. New awareness alerts and a real\u2011time whale notification area off Southern California are designed to help ships steer clear of clusters of large whales. A protected area around the Maui\u2011Nui region was expanded, and special reporting is required in humpback waters. If an animal is hurt or killed, explosives must stop immediately, and operations near live strandings must shut down. The rule also requires robust monitoring, annual public reporting, and adaptive management so measures can change as new science emerges.\n\nWho benefits: The military gets regulatory certainty to maintain readiness in a busy training range, with clearer, more predictable environmental rules. The new measures should reduce the likelihood and severity of impacts on marine mammals, especially in key breeding and feeding areas. Scientists and the public get more data and transparency, including yearly roll\u2011ups of sonar and explosives use and effectiveness of mitigation.\n\nWho could be negatively affected: Some animals will still be disturbed by noise, and a small number may be injured. NOAA anticipates up to nine large whale deaths from ship strikes over seven years, and a limited number of non\u2011fatal injuries from blasts in certain dolphin and seal populations. Conservation groups pushed for broader speed limits, larger stand\u2011off distances, and stricter behavior thresholds; many of those requests were not adopted due to safety, practicality, or mission impacts. Sensitive, resident populations around the Hawaiian Islands remain a particular concern, even with the added protections.\n\nBottom line: NOAA found the overall impact, with these safeguards, is \u201cnegligible\u201d for all affected species, and it issued a companion finding under the endangered species law that the action will not jeopardize listed animals or destroy their critical habitat. The rule adds important protections and monitoring, but it also banks on continued vigilance, better data, and the ability to adjust course if new information shows more is needed.",
